WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.880
Confidential Computing protects your data in&nbsp;
use through hardware based trusted execution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.760
environments, otherwise called enclaves, but&nbsp;
how do you adopt these secure enclaves to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.160
truly protect your applications end to end?&nbsp;
How does this align with your container or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.360
Kubernetes strategy? And do you need new skills&nbsp;
or additional DevOps workflows to adopt them?  
 &nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.760
Hi, I am Pratheek Karnati, Security Architect&nbsp;
for IBM Cloud. Today, we are going to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:29.600
Confidential Computing, and specifically, how&nbsp;
easy it is to adopt TEEs and to protect your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.120
cloud workloads using IBM Cloud Data Shield.  
 
Briefly, let’s talk about Confidential Computing…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.800
At a high-level confidential computing protects&nbsp;
data through encryption and isolation of memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.160
which complements your data in transit encryption&nbsp;
(using TLS) and at rest encryption (envelope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.760
encryption using a root key) for a true end&nbsp;
to end data protection. And this cryptographic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:56.080
isolation of memory happens either at VM or&nbsp;
at process level. You can think of it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.680
VM level cryptographic isolation or a process&nbsp;
level cryptographic isolation. For today, let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:08.320
focus on process level cryptographic solutions…  
 
…or secure enclaves… let’s look at Intel Software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.400
Guard Extensions (SGX) and how Data Shield can&nbsp;
help you adopt Intel SGX with no code changes! 
 &nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.040
Intel Software Guard Extensions (SGX) is a set&nbsp;
of security-related instruction codes that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.160
built into some modern Intel central processing&nbsp;
units (CPUs). They allow user-level as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.080
as operating system code to define private regions&nbsp;
of memory, again enclaves, whose contents are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:36.640
protected and unable to be either read or saved by&nbsp;
any process outside the enclave itself, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.920
processes running at higher privilege levels.  
 
So, where do you begin? 
 &nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.080
First, you start with the Kubernetes cluster. You&nbsp;
can provision either an IBM Kubernetes Service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:54.080
or Red Hat Open Shift on IBM Cloud cluster&nbsp;
with Intel SGX capable worker nodes. Next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.280
you install Data Shield helm chart on your&nbsp;
cluster. This installs your own dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:04.960
single tenant Data Shield instance on your&nbsp;
cluster, which includes an UI and an API.  
 &nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.080
The API seamlessly aligns with your cloud&nbsp;
native strategy and integrates with your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:16.800
DevOps pipelines to convert your container images&nbsp;
to Intel SGX capable secure enclave counterparts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.680
using industry’s best Runtime Encryption&nbsp;
technology from Fortanix! Data Shield pulls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.640
your container image from your registry, converts&nbsp;
and pushes the new image back to your registry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.400
without you having to make any code changes! You&nbsp;
can now update your Kubernetes deployment yaml&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.840
and deploy your new runtime memory&nbsp;
encrypted application to your cluster.&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.360
And yes, Data Shield also takes the&nbsp;
complexity away from attestation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.920
and presents you the attestation report&nbsp;
through a Public Key Infrastructure native to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.240
your cluster. This gives you the additional&nbsp;
layer of technical assurance you need.  
 &nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.080
The UI helps with user access management,&nbsp;
presents you with the attestation reports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.840
gives you privileges to allow or deny a certain&nbsp;
enclave, and even has an intuitive tool to convert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.760
your application for a quick demo or a PoC. 
 
To recap, Data Shield is a single tenant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.200
software that runs on your Kubernetes cluster&nbsp;
and helps you adopt Intel SGX secure enclaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.840
with technical assurance through attestation&nbsp;
reports and with no code changes.

